Ennio Morricone was an Italian composer who created music for hundreds of films.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/06/movies/ennio-morricone-dead.html|title=Ennio Morricone, Oscar-Winning Composer of Film Scores, Dies at 91|first=Robert D.|last=McFadden|author-link=Robert D. McFadden|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|date=July 10, 2020}}</ref> In the 1960s, director Sergio Leone was impressed by a musical arrangement of Morricone's and asked his former schoolmate to compose music for one of his films, ''[[A Fistful of Dollars]]''. This led to a collaboration between the two on future Leone films, many of which came to be referred to as "[[Spaghetti Western]]s".

After a steady percussion beat, the theme to ''The Good, the Bad and the Ugly'' begins with a two-note melody sounding like the howl of a coyote. Additional sounds follow, some of which [[Leitmotif|symbolize characters and themes from the film]]. This instrumental composition plays at the beginning of the film. Morricone commented that his frequent collaborator guitarist [[Bruno Battisti D'Amario]] was "able to conjure up extraordinary sounds with his guitar" recording the composition.<ref>{{cite book|editor-first=Alessandro|editor-last=De Rosa|first=Ennio|last=Morricone|title=Ennio Morricone: In His Own Words|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2veFDwAAQBAJ&pg=PT80|date=1 February 2019|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=978-0-19-068103-6|pages=80, 330}}</ref> The music is complex and features soprano recorder, drums, bass ocarina, chimes, electric guitar, trumpets, whistling, and a choir.

Largely due to the memorable quality of the main theme, the film's soundtrack peaked at #4 on the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] album chart,<ref>[{{AllMusic|class=album|id=r84071/charts-awards|pure_url=yes}} ''The Good, the Bad and the Ugly'' soundtrack chart info] AllMusic.com. Retrieved 11 June 2009.</ref> and it stayed on this chart for over a year.

